@@ -0,0 +1,48 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-root-pointer-and-move-semantics-for-json-patch-applypatch
+type: decision-log
+title: "Root-pointer and move semantics for JSON Patch applyPatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6902 leaves a few cases ambiguous or only implicit, and `applyPatch` needed concrete, consistent
+answers for them:
+
+- What `add`, `remove` and `replace` do when `path` is the empty JSON Pointer (`""`), i.e. the whole
+  document.
+- How `move` (`from` → `path`) actually transforms the document, including the RFC §4.4 requirement
+  that `from` must not be a proper prefix of `path`.
+- Whether operation and patch shapes need runtime validation given `applyPatch`'s exported TypeScript
+  types already describe them.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `add` and `replace` at the root pointer replace the whole document with (a clone of) the operation's
+  `value`; this matches how both operations already behave for any other existing location ("set this
+  location's value"), and the root is itself a valid, always-"existing" location for `replace`.
+- `remove` at the root pointer throws. There is no parent container to remove the root *from*, and
+  unlike `add`/`replace` there is no sensible value to replace it with.
+- `move` is implemented exactly as the RFC frames it: a `remove` at `from` followed by an `add` at
+  `path`, using the value read from `from` before removing it. The §4.4 prefix check is still done
+  explicitly up front (string-prefix check on the raw pointer, with a `/` boundary, so `/ab` is not
+  treated as a prefix of `/abc`) rather than left to fail naturally, since remove-then-add could
+  otherwise silently "succeed" into a nonsensical result for some inputs. Implementing it as
+  remove-then-add (rather than a bespoke splice) also makes same-array reordering fall out correctly
+  for free: removing shifts later indices down by one before `add` inserts at the destination index.
+- `applyPatch`, like `resolvePointer` before it (dl-001), validates patch and operation shapes at
+  runtime regardless of the `JsonPatch`/`JsonPatchOperation` types, and keeps throwing plain `Error`s.
+  The types document the well-formed shape for typed callers; they do not help at runtime once data has
+  come from `JSON.parse` or an external payload, which is what "throws on an invalid patch" is actually
+  for.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers can use `add`/`replace` at `""` as a "replace the whole document" operation; `remove` at `""`
+  is reserved as an error rather than silently producing `undefined` or `null`.
+- `move`/`copy` always deep-clone the value at its destination (via the shared `add` path), so a moved
+  or copied subtree is never aliased with the remainder of the result document.
+- No new error subclasses were introduced; a later feature that needs to distinguish failure kinds
+  programmatically (e.g. "invalid patch" vs. "operation failed" vs. "test failed") will still need to
+  introduce them then, same caveat as dl-001.
