@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+---
+id: task-002-implement-rfc-6902-json-patch-applypatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement RFC 6902 JSON Patch applyPatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902), with its six operations (add, remove, replace, move, copy, test), and
+export `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies the patch to a JSON document and
+returns the result as a new value without mutating its input; it throws when the patch or one of its
+operations is invalid.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Extended `src/index.test.ts` first (red): one happy-path and at least one error-path test per
+  operation, plus patch-level validation (not an array, operation not an object, unknown `op`, missing
+  `path`/`value`/`from`, malformed pointer), a no-mutation/no-aliasing test, and a multi-operation
+  sequence. Confirmed the suite failed to even import the missing `applyPatch` export.
+- Implemented `applyPatch` in `src/index.ts` (green), reusing `JsonValue`, the existing pointer
+  tokenizer (`parsePointer`) and `resolvePointer`/`resolveToken` from the RFC 6901 work:
+  - Refactored the array-index check shared by `resolveToken` and the new mutators into
+    `parseArrayIndex(token, length, allowEnd)`, since `add` additionally allows inserting one past the
+    end (including via `"-"`), while `remove`/`replace`/navigation require an existing element.
+  - `add`/`remove`/`replace` navigate to the parent container with `resolveToken` (so the parent must
+    already exist; JSON Patch never creates intermediate parents) and mutate it in place via
+    `insertAtToken`/`removeAtToken`/`replaceAtToken`. `add` and `replace` at the root pointer (`""`)
+    replace the whole document; `remove` at the root throws, since there is no parent to remove it
+    from.
+  - `move` rejects a `from` that is a proper prefix of `path` (moving a value into one of its own
+    children), per RFC 6902 §4.4, then is implemented exactly as the RFC describes: a `remove` at
+    `from` followed by an `add` at `path` with the extracted value — this also makes reordering within
+    the same array (indices shifting after the removal) fall out correctly without special-casing it.
+  - `copy` is a `resolvePointer` read followed by the same `add`; `test` is a `resolvePointer` read
+    compared with a hand-written `deepEqual` (order-independent for object keys, order-dependent for
+    arrays, since JSON patches may come from untyped/parsed JSON).
+  - Every patch application deep-clones the input document once up front (`clone`), and clones each
+    operation's `value` only at the point it is inserted into the result tree, so the original document
+    and the patch's own value objects are never aliased into the result.
+  - Patch and operation shapes are validated at runtime regardless of the exported `JsonPatch`/
+    `JsonPatchOperation` types, matching `resolvePointer`'s existing defensive-validation style, since
+    real callers pass data parsed from JSON (untyped at runtime). Plain `Error`s throughout, consistent
+    with `resolvePointer` and dl-001's choice not to introduce custom error types yet.
+- `npm test`: 54/54 passing; `node --test --experimental-test-coverage`: 100% line/branch/function
+  coverage on `src/index.ts` (no regression from the 100% baseline `resolvePointer` left). `npm run
+  typecheck` and `npm run build` both pass.
+
+## Follow-up for later features
+
+`JsonPatchOperation`/`JsonPatch` are now exported alongside `JsonValue`; later pointer-based features
+can reuse them instead of redefining operation shapes.
